.TH FKSTRNEW 3 2026-09-24 "fkstring" "fkstring Library Manual"
.nh
.SH NAME
fkstrnew, fkstrnewb, fkstrdup, fkstrdestroy \- create, copy, and destroy fkstrings
.SH LIBRARY
fkstring library
.RI ( libfkstring ,\~ \-lfkstring )
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <fkstring.h>
.PP
.BI "fkstring *fkstrnew(const char *" s );
.BI "fkstring *fkstrnewb(const void *" buf ", size_t " len );
.BI "fkstring *fkstrdup(const fkstring *" fks );
.PP
.BI "void fkstrdestroy(fkstring *" fks );
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR fkstrnew ()
creates an
.B fkstring
holding a copy of the NUL-terminated string
.IR s .
If
.I s
is NULL or empty, the result is an empty
.BR fkstring .
.PP
.BR fkstrnewb ()
creates an
.B fkstring
holding a copy of the
.I len
bytes at
.IR buf .
The bytes need not be NUL-terminated and may include NUL bytes,
which become part of the string; use it for binary data.
If
.I buf
is NULL or
.I len
is 0, the result is an empty
.BR fkstring .
.PP
.BR fkstrdup ()
creates an
.B fkstring
holding a copy of
.IR fks ,
embedded NUL bytes included.
.PP
.BR fkstrdestroy ()
frees
.I fks
and its buffer.
As with
.BR free (3),
.I fks
must not be used afterwards, and a NULL
.I fks
is ignored.
.SH RETURN VALUE
.BR fkstrnew ()
and
.BR fkstrnewb ()
return a new
.BR fkstring .
.BR fkstrdup ()
returns a new
.BR fkstring ,
or NULL if
.I fks
is NULL.
.PP
The new buffer is sized for future growth (see
.BR fkstring (3)),
so
.I alloc
is usually larger than
.IR len "\~+\~1."
.SH ERRORS
These functions do not return on allocation failure; see
.BR fkstring (3).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fkstring (3),
.BR fkalloc (3),
.BR fksprintf (3),
.BR fksubstr (3)
